[[File:Yamagata Coastline.jpg|thumb|right|300px|The eastern coastline of Yamagata Prefecture, with commuter rail lines visible along the shore.]]

'''Yamagata Prefecture''' ({{nihongo|山形県|Yamagata-ken}}) is a [[prefectures of Okaiken|prefecture]] of [[Okaiken]] located on the eastern coast of [[Harujima]] island, immediately adjacent to the [[Okami Metropolis]]. It is bordered by [[Iwate Prefecture]] to the north and the capital metropolis to the south, with the [[Sea of Harujima]] forming its eastern coastline. The prefecture is distinguished by its role as the primary '''commuter belt''' for the national capital, with thousands of residents traveling daily to work in Okami.

The capital and largest city is [[Yamagata]], with a population of approximately 723,965 residents. The prefecture has a total population of 986,192 (2026), making it one of the most densely populated prefectures outside the capital region. Yamagata Prefecture is known for its '''light manufacturing sector''', serving as a production hub for goods consumed in the capital, and its distinctive '''festival traditions''', including the famous shrine run and hand-held firework celebrations.

==History==
{{Main|History of Yamagata Prefecture}}

===Early Settlement and the Capital's Shadow===

[[File:Yamagata Historic District.jpg|thumb|right|250px|Preserved merchant houses in Yamagata's historic district, dating from the Edo period.]]

The Yamagata region has historically existed in the shadow of the capital, serving as a breadbasket and logistics hub for the imperial court since the [[Heiji period]] (794–1206). The fertile coastal plains and proximity to the capital made Yamagata an ideal location for agricultural estates supplying rice, vegetables, and seafood to the growing urban center.

During the [[Honda Shogunate]] (1601–1870), Yamagata was designated as part of the "Capital Defense Zone," with strict regulations on development to maintain clear approaches to Okami. The region's farmers held privileged status as direct suppliers to the shogunal household, and the post town of [[Nagahama]] developed as a crucial transit point for goods flowing into the capital.

===Industrialization and the Commuter Era===

The [[Saito Restoration]] (1871) transformed Yamagata from an agricultural hinterland into an industrial satellite. The construction of the first rail line connecting Okami to [[Tokuyama]] in 1876 passed through Yamagata, and the prefecture quickly developed as a manufacturing center producing consumer goods for the capital market.

The post-[[Great Adonian War]] era saw the most dramatic transformation. As Okami's population exploded during the economic boom of the 1960s and 1970s, housing prices in the capital soared beyond the reach of working families. Yamagata's coastal communities, connected by the expanding rail network, became attractive bedroom communities. The term "Yamagata commuter" ({{nihongo|山形通勤者|Yamagata-tsūkinsha}}) entered common usage, describing the thousands of workers who traveled daily to the capital.

===Modern Development===

The extension of the [[Harujima Shinkansen]] through Yamagata in 1967 cemented the prefecture's role as a commuter haven. The [[Yamagata Industrial Park]], established in 1974, attracted light manufacturing operations producing electronics, food products, and consumer goods primarily for the Okami market. Today, Yamagata maintains its dual identity as both a residential suburb and a production center for the capital region.

==Geography==

Yamagata Prefecture occupies a relatively narrow strip of coastal land along the eastern shore of Harujima island. The prefecture is bounded by the [[Sea of Harujima]] to the east, with approximately 180 kilometers of coastline featuring numerous small harbors and sandy beaches. The western boundary rises into the '''Yamagata Hills''', a low mountain range that separates the prefecture from the inland prefectures.

===Topography===

The prefecture's terrain consists primarily of '''coastal alluvial plains''', formed by sediment deposited by numerous small rivers flowing from the western hills. This flat topography facilitated the development of extensive rail infrastructure and made the region ideal for both agriculture and suburban development. The highest point, [[Mount Goshiki]] (892 m), sits on the western border and serves as a local landmark visible from much of the prefecture.

The coastline features the '''Yamagata Riviera''', a stretch of developed beachfront that has become popular with capital residents seeking weekend escapes. The resorts of [[Futami]] and [[Misaki]] attract tourists year-round, contributing to the prefecture's service economy.

