Yamagata Prefecture has a '''tropical savanna climate''' (Köppen ''Aw''), moderated by the warm waters of the Sea of Harujima. The climate is slightly drier than surrounding prefectures due to the rain shadow effect of the western hills, making it attractive for those seeking relief from the capital's humidity.

The prefecture experiences a distinct '''dry season''' from December through February, with the wet season spanning May through September. Annual precipitation averages 2,080mm, lower than neighboring coastal prefectures.

==Economy==

[[File:Yamagata Industrial Park.jpg|thumb|left|250px|The Yamagata Industrial Park, home to light manufacturing serving the capital market.]]

Yamagata Prefecture's economy is defined by its relationship with the adjacent [[Okami Metropolis]]. The prefecture functions as both a residential suburb and a production center, with most economic activity oriented toward servicing the capital's needs. The prefecture's GDP in 2025 was estimated at ε58.2 billion, with per capita income slightly above the national average due to the high proportion of professional commuters.

===Commuter Economy===

Approximately 35% of Yamagata's working population commutes daily to Okami, representing one of the highest commuter rates in Okaiken. The [[Yamagata Commuter Line]] and the [[Harujima Shinkansen]] provide rapid transit to the capital, with journey times as short as 25 minutes from central Yamagata. This commuter flow has driven extensive residential development, particularly in the southern cities nearest the metropolis.

===Manufacturing===

Light manufacturing dominates the prefecture's industrial base, with the [[Yamagata Industrial Park]] serving as the primary hub. Major sectors include food processing for the capital market, electronics assembly producing consumer devices for Okami retailers, and textile and apparel manufacturing serving the capital's fashion industry. The industrial park employs approximately 45,000 workers directly and attracts additional suppliers and service providers.

===Tourism and Services===

The '''Yamagata Riviera''' coastal resort area generates significant tourism revenue, particularly during summer months when capital residents seek beach escapes. The resorts of [[Futami]], [[Misaki]], and [[Mikame]] offer hotels, restaurants, and recreational facilities. Service industries supporting both commuters and tourists have grown substantially, with retail, healthcare, and education sectors expanding to serve the growing residential population.
Yamagata's manufacturing sector has developed high-value specializations in precision subsea robotics and optical sensor housings. Located within the Yamagata Industrial Park, independent mid-tier machine shops fabricate titanium-cased servo actuators, passive heat exchangers, and fiber-optic junction boxes designed to withstand the crushing four-hundred-atmosphere pressures of the Shendan Trench. These precision components supply both domestic deep-sea mining vessels and the subsea immersion data pods operated by Kizan Abyssal Computing, creating a highly skilled technical workforce that bridges highland mechanical engineering with oceanic exploration.

==Demographics==

Yamagata Prefecture has a population of 986,192 (2026), distributed across 21 municipalities. The population has grown steadily as Okami's housing pressures push more families into the commuter belt. The prefecture has a relatively young demographic compared to national averages, with many families attracted by more affordable housing and coastal amenities.

